## 4. Cost, stated honestly (12 October 2026)

| Point set | Points | Values per point | Houdini size (P float3 + float values) | Date |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cavity + Specular (shared positions) | 1 927 924 | 2 | 38.6 MB | 12 October 2026 |
| Roughness | 160 000 | 1 | 2.6 MB | 12 October 2026 |
| Bump | 126 337 | 1 | 2.0 MB | 12 October 2026 |
| NormalR + NormalG (shared positions) | 2 358 606 | 2 | 47.2 MB | 12 October 2026 |
| Total | 4 572 867 | | about 90 MB (about 27 MB as uint32 index + uint8 values) | 12 October 2026 |

The union of all positions covers 71.5 percent of the 4 194 304 pixels, and the total point count equals 1.09 full images. In plain words (12 October 2026): this set IS a stored image, in a less efficient form. The four 8-bit JPG-level maps that carry the pixel content (Cavity, NormalR, NormalG, plus the Specular/Bump/Roughness corrections) weigh about 4 MB each raw, while the point clouds cost 90 MB in Houdini. The derivations (Gloss, Specular, Bump bands, Normal B) are genuine and cheap; the pixel residual of Cavity and Normal is not compressible by geometry. (12 October 2026)

## 5. Copernicus nodes the layer would need (12 October 2026)

- Derivations (12 October 2026): `cop/remap` for Gloss = 1 - Roughness and Specular = a*Cavity + b; 8 `cop/blur` plus 8 subtract/weighted `cop/blend` for the displacement bands that feed Bump; `cop/derivative` (or a 1 px offset and subtract) on the bands for the Normal gradient terms; `cop/channeljoin` plus a small expression for B = sqrt(1 - R2 - G2). (12 October 2026)
- Point corrections (12 October 2026): points carrying the residual values (measured vector data, loaded from file in SOPs), rasterised to a 2048 grid at 1 px by a SOP Volume Rasterize Attributes or by 1 px quads in `cop/rasterizegeo` (quads multiply the element count by 4 points + 1 primitive), brought in through `cop/geotolayer-2.0`, then added to the base with `cop/blend` (add). No COP Blur softening may be applied to these layers, since the content lives below 1 px. (12 October 2026)

## 6. Verdict (12 October 2026)

Derivation alone settles three maps: Gloss (from Roughness), Specular (from Cavity, plus 26 169 shared-position points once Cavity is at 0.995) and Bump (from the displacement bands, plus 126 337 points because our displacement lacks its 1 to 4 px detail). It cannot settle Cavity or the Normal, which are independent of the 2K height at pixel scale (best derivation 0.51 and 0.52). The smallest set that must be reproduced at pixel level is therefore {Cavity, NormalR, NormalG}, and reaching 0.99 on it costs 4.57 million exact points (about 90 MB in Houdini), which is equivalent to storing about one full image and therefore breaks the spirit of the no-image rule even though each point is measured vector data. Reaching 0.99 with a non-image representation would require a generative model of the 8K micro-grain, which Pearson at full resolution cannot reward, since it demands exact phase. (12 October 2026)
